- **Equal steps are not equal compute.** From the architecture sizes (my arithmetic, approximate, ignoring layer norm, softmax and elementwise work): one forward pass for one window costs about 0.31 M multiply-accumulates for the LSTM (72 recurrent steps × 4,224 + head) and about 0.56 M for attention (Q/K/V projections 0.22 M, the 72×72 score and weighting products 0.33 M, head). Attention is therefore roughly 1.8× more arithmetic per window, yet it was **faster** in measured time. So neither "same epochs", "same parameter count (within 11%)" nor "same wall-clock" corresponds to the same arithmetic, and each gives a different reading of "same budget". The two models also use different kernels (a sequential recurrence versus dense matrix products), so arithmetic does not map to time either.
- **Wall-clock differences are not a controlled architecture benchmark.** The same code and the same seed took very different times across runs that are otherwise bit-identical in results:

| Run (seed 0, same data and code) | Median train seconds per epoch |
|---|--:|
| LSTM, 20-epoch run | 28.8 |
| LSTM, 50-epoch run | 19.3 |
| Attention, 20-epoch run | 10.5 |
| Attention, 50-epoch run | 8.2 |

  Across the 50-epoch runs the median epoch time ranged 10.9–19.3 s for the LSTM and 4.2–8.2 s for attention, and the slowest epoch in a single run was up to eight times the median (LSTM seed 2: its first five epochs took 32, 29, 25, 44 and 85 s, then settled near 10.9 s). The runs were executed one after another at different times, not interleaved. I conclude that machine load or execution conditions changed during and between runs; the artifacts and code do not record which conditions (the thread count was not set or saved), so I do not attribute it to anything specific.
- **What can be said about time.** In all four same-seed pairs the LSTM epoch was slower than the attention epoch, by a factor between 2.3 and 2.8, which is consistent in direction, but each pair ran at a different time, so the factor is an indication and not a measurement. Mean elapsed time per run was 17:17 (LSTM) and 7:05 (attention), including validation each epoch; the SDs (76 s and 173 s) are mostly environmental.
- **Convergence was not reached** in the sense of section 3, so the required comparison "each trained to convergence" has not yet been made. A comparison of two models cut off by the same epoch cap is a comparison of how far each got in 50 epochs.

## 6. Recommended protocol for the next stage (proposal; nothing has been run)

**Status of existing artifacts.** Keep all of them untouched. Label the `epoch50` runs, the 20-epoch `final` pair and the 1-epoch `smoke` pair as **exploratory/pilot**. They informed the protocol below, so they must not be counted as the eventual comparison, and decisions made while looking at them used validation data only. New runs go to a new directory (for example `artifacts/compare/`), with exploratory and comparison runs in separate subfolders.

**Can the existing runs be continued? Not exactly.** Verified from the code and the artifacts: each file holds only the best-validation `state_dict`, the config, scaler and history. It does **not** hold Adam's moment estimates or step counter, nor any RNG state, nor the last epoch's weights (which differ from the best weights for five of six runs). `train_model` has no resume path. Two things do help: batch order is a pure function of `(seed, epoch)`, so epochs 51 onwards are reproducible, and a longer run is an exact prefix-extension of a shorter one (verified in section 1). Warm-starting from a best checkpoint with a fresh Adam would be a different procedure (a restart), not a continuation, and should not be called one. The clean option is to **rerun from scratch to a larger cap**: it reproduces epochs 1–50 exactly (same machine and library versions) and costs the 50 epochs again. Adding true resume support (save last-epoch weights and optimizer state) would be a code change and is a decision for you.

**Shared resource budget (proposed).** Define it in countable terms, identically for both models: batch size 128, a maximum of **150 epochs (157,950 steps)**, three seeds each, and the stopping rule below. Report three things side by side without merging them: optimizer steps (equal), approximate arithmetic per window (about 0.31 M versus 0.56 M MACs forward), and measured wall-clock (informational only, with machine conditions recorded). Do not call the budget "equal compute"; call it "equal step budget".

**Convergence and stopping criteria (proposed, same for both).** Evaluate validation MAE every epoch; smooth with a trailing 5-epoch mean; declare convergence when the best smoothed value over the last 20 epochs improves on the best before them by less than 0.005 °C, with a minimum of 50 epochs. Keep selecting the checkpoint by raw validation MAE as now. Record the epoch at which the rule fired, or that it did not fire. The threshold is about twice the epoch-to-epoch SD of the smoothed curve in the pilot and is deliberately small relative to the model gap; it is a starting value for you to confirm.

**Tuning allowance (proposed, same for both).** Either none (keep the pilot configuration and just train to convergence), or the same small grid for each architecture, for example three configurations (lr 0.001 constant; lr 0.001 with a validation-driven decay; lr 0.0003 constant) at one tuning seed that is not used in the comparison, each run under the same cap and rule, chosen by smoothed validation MAE. Model width is held at the current sizes unless you decide otherwise. The allowance counts as part of each model's resource budget and is reported. Learning-rate decay would need a code change, applied identically to both.

**If the budget expires before convergence.** Report both models as **budget-limited**: show the full validation curves, the flag that the best epoch is at the cap, and the improvement over the last 20 epochs; make no ranking claim that the uncertainty does not support; do not extend one model and not the other. If you extend, extend both to the same new cap under the same rule and disclose the extension and the reason.

**Estimated additional work (from the pilot timings; wide uncertainty).** Per-epoch time including validation was about 11.5–20.5 s (LSTM) and 4.5–8.9 s (attention) across the three pilot seeds, but the pilot shows run-to-run swings of 2× or more from machine conditions, and the 20-epoch run was slower than all of them. A 150-epoch run would then take roughly 29–51 min (LSTM) and 11–22 min (attention) per seed, so three seeds each is about **2.2–3.9 h (LSTM) plus 0.9–1.7 h (attention), roughly 3–6 h in total**, less if the stopping rule fires early. A three-configuration tuning allowance at one seed adds about 1.5–2.6 h for the LSTM and 0.6–1.1 h for attention. Plan for up to 2× above these if the machine is busy. These are estimates from six runs, not measurements of the proposed protocol.

**Controls to add before the comparison.** Run the models alternately (not all of one, then all of the other), on an otherwise idle machine, and record hardware, thread count, OS, start time and git commit in each artifact. These are code and procedure changes, not done here.

**Test evaluation.** Stays reserved. Run it once per final configuration, after the protocol, the grid and the stopping rule are frozen and all comparison runs have finished, and never use it to choose epochs, seeds or settings.

